bpf: arm64 JIT for l4h

The l4h aarch64 guest builds arch/arm64/net, with the A64 instruction
encoders (arch/arm64/lib/insn.c) and the arm64 headers l4h has no copy
of; arch/l4h/arm64/bpf_jit.c patches code in place.  What the JIT needs
and l4h, whose guest kernel runs at EL0, does not have:

- exception tables: a fault of the guest kernel stops the guest, so
  programs with BPF_PROBE_MEM loads are left to the interpreter (which
  checks the address first), and arena accesses are not offered;
- TPIDR_EL1 and SP_EL0: no per-CPU instructions, private stacks or
  inlined bpf_get_smp_processor_id()/bpf_get_current_task();
- the Spectre-BHB state and firmware calls, for classic BPF;
- the upper half of the address space: emit_addr_mov_i64() assumes
  kernel addresses have their top 16 bits set;
- arch_bpf_stack_walk(), for BPF exceptions;
- set_memory_*(): guest-kernel memory is writable.

insn.c gets ALIGN_DOWN from <linux/align.h> rather than through arm64's
asm/esr.h.

